y we believe that rms could be a  candidate for chimeric antigen receptor  or car t cell therapy  this therapy uses t cells engineered to  express  a receptor containing an extracellular  antibody fragment against a  tumor-associated antigen  and an intracellular  simulatory domain used to activate the t  cell as shown in this cartoon on the on  the right of the slide  car t therapy has now been tested in  many trials and has had much success in  hematological cancers  but has not shown the same responses in  solid tumors as demonstrated by the  clinical trial results summarized here  in the table  which mostly result in no objective  responses  knowing these challenges we set out to  design a car capable  of clearing rms tumors by developing a  highly effective car  then armoring these t cells against the  tumor micro environment to overcome the  unique challenges in solitude  the first step of this project was to  find and validate  target in rms  specifically we want to know which rms  specific surface proteins can be  targeted by cars  and can an rms directed car actually  control tumor growth in vivo  the rms targets were identified using  gene microarrays  comparing multiple types of pediatric  tumors against healthy tissues  first overall expressed  overall  the highly expressed transcripts were  identified  and these hits were filtered out for  cell surface proteins specifically  which is uh the only  molecules that a car would be able to  identify  and finally individually annotated for  the most likely candidates that could be  targeted by a car  from this we arrived at a much smaller  group of potential targets  so the top hits  from this process included m-cam ftfr4  activein and gpc2 along with other  several other cancer-associated genes  fgfr4 was chosen chosen as a strong  candidate that is as it is upregulated  upregulated  in both arms and erms  and is expected to have little to no  expression in healthy mature tissues  though several other candidates on this  list have also been selected for  development  to validate fgfr4 as a potential car  keeper uh target  uh my predecessors looked at fgfr4  expression  by immunohistochemistry in the top left  rna based gene expression on the bottom  left  and then total protein amounts shown on  the right  in each case we see that the tumor  tissues have much higher ftfr4  expression  than the healthy healthy tissues do  and on the right we see that even  among other tumor types ftfr4 is much  more highly expressed the rms specific  humor types shown in gray  compared to either the normal tissues  showing black or other cell lines shown  in white  this suggests that fgfr4 would be a good  car target as there's a little chance of  off tumor on target toxicity  as the healthy tissues show very low  expression of fpfr4  really work on this project led to the  development of a car that was able to  control tumors in an easier to treat  metastatic rms model  a significant reduction in the tumor  burden measured by levy vote imaging  and an increase in survival  as seen in this data here  however the same car was unable to  control intramuscular or atopic tumors  suggesting that further development  would be needed  furthermore when car t cells were  introduced  the tumors responded by producing more  stromal regions within the tumor  as seen all the way to the left in the h  and e staining  on the bottom with right on top we have  tumor alone on the bottom we have tumor  plus car t cells  you see that there's this lighter  colored strands which indicate stromal  regions and tumor  but only when we treat with car t cells  additionally the vast majority of cd8  and cd4 t cells  were localized to the stromal region  effectively blocking the car t cells  from reaching the lesion  additionally pdl1 expression was  upregulated  it's likely priming these t cells for  exhaustion this data provided early  hints about the tumor's defenses due car  t therapy that would need to be overcome  my role in this project was to improve  upon the previous cars by screening new  binding domains for ftfr4  i set out to answer whether we could  increase the efficacy of the fgfr4 car  and if this would be sufficient to  overcome the immunosuppressive  human microenvironment  working with our collaborators from  university of pittsburgh  they created  new ftf r4 binders using the phage  display library for huma fully human  binders  which included both scfvs as well as  single chain  binding  domains original bait molecule shown on  the top left  used the entire extracellular  domain of fgfr4  however we now know that targeting  membrane proximal regions  makes more effective cars  so we streamed a new bait molecule which  only included the ig-3 domain of fdfr4  and  from this collaboration we received  eight single chain binders  as well as  seven  scfe  binders  uh and the overall  structure of these is  shown in the cartoons on the left  for the seven scfbs we tested these in  both the heavy light and light heavy  chain orientations  binders were all fused to a cd8 hinge  and transmembrane domain  or 1bb costim and cd3 zeta domain  we assessed car surface antigenic  surface expression using biotinylated  ftfr protein  sustained four ftfr4 cars directly  we also assessed the cytokine production  looking specifically at interferon gamma  tnf alpha and il2 cytokines produced  in response to coca culture with target  cells  and not shown here  but we also assessed the cytotoxicity of  these cars against their target cell  lines  from this initial set of data  we selected the top five most promising  cars to further validate in vitro  interestingly we also observed  differences  in both cytokine and  cytotoxicity profiles  by changing the chain orientation from  life heavy to heavy light  is a critical step in selecting and  optimizing our cars  from our top candidates we assess the  top five  or cytotoxicity against  dense and rms cell line  specifically the rh-30 cell line  which we engineered to also express a  truncated cd19 or a positive control  on the left  we have cope we co-cultured car t cells  and rh-30 cells at varying effector to  target ratios  however we know that sarcomas are also  particularly sensitive to nk like  killing  which can show up as non-specific  killing in these ctl assays  on the right  we added unlabeled k562 cells at a 30 to  1 ratio with rh-30 cells to remove some  of this non-specific killing  from this data  we can see that two car candidates  particularly stand out  which are the 150 heavy light not 154  heavy light  so it's interesting that  of all of our candidates both of them  came from this heavy light uh category  and we're still looking into whether or  not this is a particular feature of  the architecture of the car or if this  is just a lock in the sense that these  two cars worked best  we also tested several other cell lines  for non-specific killing  we tested rajy cells showed very little  non-specific  killing by these car t cells  we also tested k562 cells  which  alone showed varying degrees of  non-specific killing  however when we used coal target  inhibition again uh with 31 to 30 to 1  ratio of  unlabeled cells  we see that most of this is non-specific  to the car  so this does give us some insight into  which of these cars are giving the  strongest off-target effects  and to test these further we did a  stress test for these five candidates  in which we repeatedly challenge the  cells every two days with additional  target cells  starting at either one to one shown on  top right graph  or a one to four ratio shown in the  bottom really graph  the cars were co-cultured with rh-30  cells for two days  and the remaining cell fractions were  determined by foil  which is what's plotted  and the original ratio is re-established  every two days  this reprocess was repeated for a total  of three challenges over six days  and as the grass on the right indicates  this assay helped us differentiate from  the car's ability to repeatedly kill the  tumor cells  and finally cytokine production of these  top candidates were tested and compared  to the original fgfr4 car  which are legal tiers m410 and m412  while several of the new cars  particularly stand out such as 146 and  151 are  all the new cars do substantially better  than the originals  giving us hope that these will have  better effects in vivo  and with all the in vitro data taken  together we chose to continue in vivo  with two of our top candidates for 154  hl and 150 150 hl  these were chosen for their specificity  improved cytokine production and ability  to respond to multiple rounds of target  cells  using nsg mice we injected rh-30 cells  expressing our truncated cd19  into the lake muscle  orthotopically  three days later we injected seven  million car t cells  either by iv or directly into the tumor  on day seven  however we still did not see any effect  of the fgfr4 cars in these orthotopic  tumors despite their improvement in  vitro  we were excited to note however that the  cd19 car used as a positive control  was able to delay tumor growth when  injected by iv or by inter-tumor  injection  this is suggested to us that the rms  tumors can be overcome but only under  the right conditions  we believe that this difference between  the fgfr4 car and the cd19 car is  largely due to the number of target  molecules present on the cell surface  the histogram on the left shows fgfr4  expression of several synchoma lines  with rh30 shown in orange  and with the quantification of the  molecules per cell  on the right  and rh 30s here compared to several  other ewing sarcoma cell lines  and so well fgfr4 is much more highly  expressed in the rmsl lines than others  it's still far  or about 30 fold lower  expression than the cd19 target on our  sellings  and this suggests that  the cv19 card is working better because  it seemed much more targeted  one way to overcome this this issue is  actually by lowering the threshold of  the fgfr4 car  so that it can work more effectively  against  a low density antigen  this might be a necessary process  for optimization strategy  for controlling rms tumors  in addition to the car t cells going  into the mice  we also need to overcome some of the  tumor defenses  to allow the fgfr4 cars to function in  vivo  for this we need to know what  defenses the tumor has  and to identify the most relevant  pathways in rms we compared rh-30 cells  in culture  with and without interfering gamma  treatments  mimic the response of t cells in vivo  the chart here shows the highest express  soluble factors  identified  and they are listed based on  rh30 treatment through  so each column here it shows a different  replicate  and the tc71  shown on the right side of this chart  was used as a uh  as a comparison  tc71 is a ewing sarcoma on  and some of the top hits that we  identified  for us to target include  macrophage migration inhibitory factor  uh  mis  beta  chemokine ccl24  leukemia inhibitory factor or lif  and i201  these soluble factors are all present in  many other tumors as well  which allowed us to build on the work  from others to target these pathways  either with small molecules  or by armoring our car  soluble factors were confirmed by elisa  assay in multiple rms cell lines so we  used rms 13 cell line rd  and rd rh30 which i showed you  previously  so here i'm showing several different  graphs of um  concentration of each of these soluble  factors after interfering gamma  treatment for either 24 or 48 hours  mif we only tested at 24 hours  and for each one of these cell lines  as you can see there's some variation in  response between cell lines  however for mif lif and tgf beta we do  see  increased amounts of these pro  these soluble factors  compared to either other tumors  or are upregulated upon interfering  gamma stimulation  so these are all likely important  pathways generalized to rms that we  should  and may have to target  and as indicated previously the  introduction of t cells into  tumor-bearing mice  causes these tumors to respond by  producing more spermal regions  on the top we have a mouse that only has  tumor on the bottom panels we have the  tumor plus t cells  unstained by h e  on the bottom you can see these long  strands of light colored fibers  indicating stromal regions within the  tumor  we mainly see these upon  t cell treatments  furthermore when these tissues are  stained using a trichrome stain  we see a bright blue stain which  indicates the presence of collagen  again this is much more  prevalent when we have activated t cells  in the  mice treated with activated t cells  now on the right here  we have immunofluorescent staining of  cd11b myeloid population  as well as cd3t cells  which are refined to be in close contact  with one another  and particularly localized within the  stromal regions  these results  paired with the previous data  in the early ftfr4 cars  suggest that these tumors are responding  to treatment by building up more stromal  regions  these regions are preventing t cells  from entering the tumor lesion itself  furthermore we know that there are  myeloid populations here so we have yet  to determine if these are  biological  derived suppressor cells  or tumor associated macrophages  so in conclusion  our early studies have defined a target  in rms to approach with car t therapy  fgfr4 is highly expressed in both fusion  positive and fusion negative rms  but not expressed in mature healthy  tissues  furthermore fdfr4 cars can control rms  tumors in a metastatic model  but not in but not yet an orthotopic  model  my role in this project is to build new  fgfr4 cars we have developed more  effective ftfr4 cars in vitro  by screening against a more membrane  proximal fragment of fgfr4  and while these new fgfr4 cars still do  not control orthotopic rms tumors  we do see that our positive control as  well  cd19 car is able to control  these tumors  suggesting that there is a potential for  cartoon therapy  or specifically rms tumors  the point i didn't elaborate on in this  too much in this talk  but important to note out is that the  intra-tumor delivery of car t cells did  not provide any benefit over the  intravenous delivery  this is likely because of the stromal  regions that develop  in the tumors  it's likely that our t cells are being  um  our t cells are weaving  tumor logician itself and being  specifically brought into stromal  regions where they have little to no  effect  on the tumor growth itself  and finally we define some of the tumor  defenses defenses that we need to  overcome  and delivery of t cells induces stromal  formation in rms tumors which prevents  the infiltration of t cells into the  tumor  likely through the action of mdscs  or tumor associated macrophages in the  resulting stroma  furthermore multiple salval soluble  factors in the tme  may prevent car t function including  gigf beta mif  lif and idol 1.  so  our next steps are to first optimize the  ftfr4car to function at a lower  threshold of target molecules  and two to overcome tumor  microenvironments i think both of these  approaches are going to be necessary in  this tumor model  we are building new tools to test these  hypotheses  we now have an mif lif  gcl24 and combined mif-lif knockout cell  lines or rms which we are testing in  vivo  additionally we are also targeting pdl1  signaling by using pd1 blocking  antibodies to see if that is sufficient  in  improving the function of these fgfr4  cars  and on the card engineering side  we're developing several new cars that  have been shown  by others who have improved in vivo  function  that will target molecule densities  as shown in this cartoon in the bottom  right  in summary rms like many other solid  tubers will take a targeted approach to  overcome  we're developing optimized cars  on with cars with multiple multiple  targets  and logic gated cars  we're also targeting many of the tumor  defenses such as tgf beta signaling  we're targeting lif mif ccl 24 soluble  factors  as well as pdl1pt1 signaling  and metabolic t-cell suppression from  idol one  in addition to these  molecular  defenses  we're also targeting  we're looking at the presence of  immunosuppressive cells such as  tumor-associated macrophages  and myeloid deprived suppressor cells  we believe that this multi-targeted  approach will be  necessary  but we'll be able to overcome rms tumors  may give hope for the sound therapy  where they're most desperately  desperately needed  finally i'd like to thank the many  people who contributed to this project  well as well as the funding sources  thank you for your attention

